---
description: Avoid obvious, redundant, or code-narrating comments
alwaysApply: true
---

# No Obvious Comments

Do not add comments that are obvious, already explained by the code, or redundant. Let clear code and good naming speak for itself.

Only add a comment when the code is genuinely not self-explanatory and truly requires very brief context (the "why", a non-obvious trade-off, or a constraint). This should be rare.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — narrates what the code already says
// Import the router
import { useRouter } from '@/navigation';

// Increment the counter
count += 1;

// Loop over items
items.forEach((item) => render(item));

// ✅ GOOD — no comment needed; code is self-explanatory
count += 1;

// ✅ GOOD — explains a non-obvious "why", kept brief
// Safari clamps this below 16px, so bump to avoid iOS zoom-on-focus
fontSize: '16px';
```
